//? Title: Nearest-neighbor interpolation
//? Subtitle: Actual binary searches, midpoint ties and the original smooth-function staircase
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Estimate a sampled function at a new coordinate by returning the ordinate of its
nearest sample. This preserves existing values without inventing averages, at
the cost of jumps between sample regions. We inspect actual binary-search
decisions, retain the original nine-node smooth-function example, and check a
Lipschitz error reference using a prepared native model with complete input
validation.
)

\toc

> Nearest samples define one-dimensional cells

For strictly ordered nodes x0 through x[n−1], each neighboring pair shares its
midpoint as a decision boundary. The lower index owns an exact distance tie.
Outside the sample range, the nearest endpoint is selected. This produces a
piecewise-constant function that reproduces all nodes exactly and returns only
stored ordinates. Numeric values can encode categories, but averaging or
smoothing is absent. Discontinuities can be undesirable for a smooth signal; the
method is not universally artifact-free or fastest on every workload.

The producer first tests the two endpoints, then binary-searches the first node
not below an interior query and compares its distance with the preceding node's
distance. Every iteration shrinks the inclusive search interval, giving
logarithmic search work. The prepared model validates and owns the complete
nodes once. The one-query convenience function performs that validation/setup on
every call, so its full cost is linear in node count rather than just its search
cost.

> Three samples and a hand-checkable tie

For nodes0,2,5 and ordinates10,20,30, query1 is equally far from0 and2 and
chooses the lower sample. The diagram and table use actual inclusive bounds and
midpoint comparisons from the canonical producer. The widened final distances
are recorded separately; the report does not replay binary search.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nearest_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/nearest_neighbor_interpolation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::NearestNeighborInterpolant model({0, 2, 5}, {10, 20, 30});
    auto t = model.trace(1);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("| actual lo | actual hi | actual mid | compared abscissa | go right |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: " "| ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.steps.size(); ++k) {
        auto &s = t.steps[k];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.lo, s.hi, s.mid,
                   s.abscissa, s.go_right);
        auto id = std::to_string(k);
        d.node(id, {6.0 * k, 0},
               std::format("Actual bounds {}..{}\nxs[{}]={} / go right {}",
                           s.lo, s.hi, s.mid, s.abscissa, s.go_right));
        if (k)
            d.edge(std::to_string(k - 1), id);
    }
    std::print("\nActual distances: {} and {}. Selected sample: {}, value: {}.\n",
               t.left_distance, t.right_distance, t.selected, t.value);
    d.save_svg("build/nearest_tiny.svg");
}
```
