> Derive and check an error reference

If the underlying function is L-Lipschitz, its value changes by at most L times
coordinate distance. Inside a covered interval of width h, the nearest node is
at distance at most h/2. Thus absolute interpolation error is at most Lh/2 in
exact arithmetic. For sin(x)+0.3x, the derivative is cos(x)+0.3 with absolute
value at most1.3, so the uniform-grid reference is1.3h/2. This bound applies
inside the covered domain, not arbitrary extrapolation or floating roundoff.

$$
|f(x)-f(x_{nearest})|\le L|x-x_{nearest}|\le Lh/2.
$$
\alttext(Lipschitz continuity bounds nearest-sample error by one-half of the interval width times the Lipschitz constant.)

The next native studies refine the same domain with9,17,33 and65 nodes. Each
table row reports the actual maximum only on its fixed finite query grid.
Agreement supports the finite experiment; the Lipschitz argument supplies the
domain-wide exact-arithmetic statement.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nearest_error.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/nearest_neighbor_interpolation.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, error, bound;
    std::print("| sample nodes | native maximum finite-grid error | Lipschitz reference |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t n : {9U, 17U, 33U, 65U}) {
        auto s = dm::algorithms::nearest_neighbor_demo_study(n, 1280);
        x.push_back(6.4 / (n - 1));
        error.push_back(s.max_grid_error);
        bound.push_back(s.error_reference);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", n, s.max_grid_error, s.error_reference);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, error, "observed grid error", {37, 99, 235}, 6).line(
        x, bound, "Lipschitz reference", {220, 38, 38}, 2).title(
        "Nearest samples: finite-grid error and linear reference").x_label(
        "sample interval width h").y_label("absolute error").save_svg(
        "build/nearest_error.svg");
}
```
